Round-3 B1 — the mechanism now exists, and is NOT a gate. BLOCKING.
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The narrow finding is closed: staleCertificateError is reached for the
population merge actually operates on, and it fires.

  lab2, studio INSIDE the repo (the real layout), strategy rebase, opus done,
  certificate written by the real `bisellium verify` on the opus branch:
    opus/W-900 was rebased onto master — opus W-900's gate "tests" certifies
    tree:39b11532..., which no longer matches the rebased tree
    (tree:c3dd5809...); re-run "bisellium verify W-900" and retry the merge
    exit 1, master untouched at "trunk moved", branch left in place.
  lab4, same under push + pr.required with a live origin: refusal precedes the
  push; origin's opus branch and master both untouched. Correct ordering.

B1.4 (new, blocking). The refusal is a one-shot warning, not a gate: running
the same command a second time lands the work with the same stale
certificate.

  lab3, nothing done between the two invocations — no verify, no edit:
    merge #1  -> refuses, "...certifies tree:deadbeef..., which no longer
                 matches the rebased tree (tree:c3dd5809...); re-run
                 'bisellium verify W-900' and retry the merge"   exit 1
    merge #2  -> "merged opus/W-900 into master"                 exit 0
                 master: 3a1f339 feat: feature / 9d8e4d4 trunk moved / init
                 certificate still on record: tree:deadbeefdeadbeef...
  and no note at all on run #2, because `rebased` is false.

  Cause: the check sits inside `if (mergeBase !== masterRev) { if rebase }`
  (branch.ts:271-283). The first run performs the rebase and is not rolled
  back, so on the second run mergeBase == masterRev, the whole block is
  skipped, and merge goes straight to landing/pushing. The commit message
  notes "a retry after verify needs no second rebase" — the same property
  also means the retry gets no second CHECK.

  Under the studio's own configuration this is worse than local-only (lab4):
    merge #1 -> refuses on the stale certificate, exit 1
    merge #2 -> exit 0, force-with-lease pushes the rebased branch to origin
                and reports the PR ready; certificate still tree:deadbeef...
  i.e. the error message's own instruction ("retry the merge") is sufficient
  to defeat the refusal, and the second run is silent about it.

B1.5 (new, advisory-to-blocking, same mechanism). The remedy the error names
produces a certificate for the WRONG tree.

  `verify` certifies `--commit`, default HEAD, in the repo it is pointed at.
  `merge` hashes the OPUS BRANCH. Run from a trunk checkout — the natural
  place to run merge, and what CLAUDE.md's own `bisellium verify <opus>
  --studio studio --repo .` produces — they disagree by construction.
  lab4, HEAD on master, branch rebased, merge having reported the rebased
  tree as tree:98cf84c5...:
    bisellium verify W-900 --studio ... --repo ...          -> certifies
      tree:489d8010...   (master's tree — NOT what ships)
    bisellium verify W-900 ... --commit opus/W-900          -> certifies
      tree:98cf84c5...   (matches merge)
  The error names the first. The second is never mentioned — and it certifies
  a tree the gate commands were not run against, since verify executes them in
  the working tree. The honest remedy is "check out opus/<id>, then verify";
  neither the message nor ADOPTION.md says so. lab2 confirms the consequence:
  following the message verbatim, the retry succeeded carrying a certificate
  for master's tree.

On the design call (refuse vs re-run) — refuse is right
-------------------------------------------------------
Endorsed. Re-running gates inside merge would duplicate verify's dirty-tree
refusal, its evidence writing and its certificate format in a second place,
and merge would then be the one command that both moves refs and executes
arbitrary manifest commands. One place runs gates. D-015 itself offers both
answers and this is the cheaper, more honest one — a merge that says "this is
not what was certified" is a fact it can establish without running anything.
Reusing sourceTreeHash rather than re-deriving it is the right call too, and
the exclude set matches check.ts/verify.ts line for line.

The objection is not to the choice, it is that as built the refusal is
advice with an exit code. For it to be the answer D-015 asks for, the
comparison has to be evaluated for the tree that is about to ship on every
run that lands or pushes — not only on the run that happens to perform the
rebase. That also removes the fast_forward blind spot for free: today an
ff-able branch whose certificate predates its last commit lands with no
word, which is the same defect the second invocation exercises.

Advisories
----------
A4.1  pull_after_push inert under pr.required — accepted. Documented
      accurately in ADOPTION.md ("with pr.required: true, pull_after_push
      never runs"). Reading a setting and saying where it does not apply is
      the right resolution for a seam W-028 will move.

A4.2  Invalid strategy: silently degrading — I accept leaving the fallback
      (check.ts:272-287 does block it at manifest.shape; verified by reading
      the rule). I do NOT accept the sentence that justifies it. ADOPTION.md
      now says of a typo'd strategy: "a typo here is not yet caught
      anywhere". That is false and contradicts the defence the builder gave
      for keeping the fallback — `bisellium check` blocks it:
      `strategy "<x>" must be one of fast_forward, rebase, merge_commit`.
      A doc line that tells the operator a rule does not exist when it does
      is the same class of defect as round 3's B1 note. Fix the sentence.

A4.3  Brief scoping — honest, not rationalised, with one gap. Files owned now
      declares check.ts, check.test.ts, adapters/native/src/index.ts and both
      manifests, which is the whole diff except docs/ADOPTION.md (undeclared,
      and only touched because round 3 asked for it) and the brief itself.
      But only "Files owned" was amended: "Behaviours to test" still lists 1-7
      while the PR's evidence rests on 10-21, and the Intent still says merge
      "fast-forwards master when the opus is done", which D-015 superseded.
      Half an amendment.

A4.4  The exclude set is unpinned by any test. Mutation M-E replaced
      sourceExcludeDirs's body with `return []` — full branch suite 0 FAIL.
      Behaviours 18/19 use a studio OUTSIDE the repo, where the exclusion is
      inert either way, so nothing holds branch.ts's notion of "the tree" to
      verify.ts's. If they ever drift the failure is silent and total: every
      certificate mismatches and every rebase merge refuses. One behaviour
      with the studio inside the repo and a bookkeeping-only trunk commit
      (which must NOT refuse) would pin it.

A4.5  A refused merge still leaves the branch rebased and, when it had a
      remote counterpart, local and origin diverged (lab4: origin a1f1acd,
      local rebased). Deliberate per the commit message, and benign given
      force-with-lease — but it means "refuses" is not "no side effects", and
      it is what makes B1.4 reachable.

A4.6  certifies: dirty:<hash> is ignored (startsWith("tree:") only), so an
      opus certified with --allow-dirty passes the new check silently.
      Defensible; worth a line in ADOPTION.md rather than only in code.

