        proves it: the FAIL message reads "certifies tree:6c9da187..., which
        no longer matches the rebased tree (tree:6c9da187...)" — the two
        hashes are IDENTICAL, which only the described always-mismatch
        mutation can produce. I reproduced it: mutation M-B2 (`certifies !==
        newHash` -> `true`) gives the same 2 FAIL, same assertions, same
        message shape. Accepted as a mutation record rather than a
        pre-implementation red; the log itself does not say which it is, and
        should.
20.log  2 PASS + 1 FAIL ("the failure still carries the rebase note
        undefined") — the module loaded, the suite ran, only the new
        assertion fails.
21.log  3 FAIL with the real git output:
        ! [rejected] opus/W-093 -> opus/W-093 (non-fast-forward).
No two cover the same behaviour (note text / refusal fires / refusal does not
false-positive / note on the delete path / force-with-lease). All five carry
bisellium red's header with command, exit, timestamp and tree.

Mutation — the suite bites, with one hole
-----------------------------------------
Six mutations of branch.ts, each reverted and the file diffed clean against a
saved copy; `branch.test.ts .` re-run per mutation.
  M-A  staleCertificateError neutered (gates -> undefined)        4 FAIL (18)
  M-B2 comparison forced to always-mismatch                       2 FAIL (19)
       — reproduces red 19 exactly
  M-C  forceWithLease dropped from the pr.required push           3 FAIL (21)
  M-D  note: staleNote dropped from the delete-failure path       1 FAIL (20)
  M-F  hash the trunk instead of the opus branch                  2 FAIL (19)
  M-E  sourceExcludeDirs -> return []                             0 FAIL  <-- hole
M-A, M-B2 and M-F are the ones asked for: the new refusal is pinned in both
directions (fires on mismatch, does not fire on match) and the ref it hashes
is pinned. M-E is A4.4.

Verdict
-------
FAIL, round 4. One blocking finding, and it is the same finding as round 3 in
a new place: the mechanism D-015 rests on does not cover the population it has
to gate. Round 3 it could not fire at all; now it fires once, then steps aside
for the next invocation of the same command — the one its own error message
tells the operator to run.

B2, A2, A7 are genuinely closed and were re-derived rather than taken on
trust. The design call is right and I would not have it re-run gates. The
refusal needs to be evaluated per-merge against the tree about to ship rather
than per-rebase, and its error needs to name a remedy that produces a matching
certificate.

